## Abstract
This paper presents the implementation of concepts related to Software Defined Radio (SDR) using GNU Radio. The work included the use of GitHub repositories in a collaborative environment and the development of Python blocks such as accumulator, differentiator, and statistical operators for signal analysis in the time domain. These implementations strengthened the understanding of digital communication systems in real time and demonstrated the flexibility of GNU Radio for the design and evaluation of signal processing systems.  

**Keywords—** SDR, GNU Radio, signal processing, accumulator, differentiator, statistics.

---

## I. Introduction
Software Defined Radio (SDR) plays a fundamental role in modern telecommunications due to its flexibility and adaptability. A single platform can support multiple standards and handle a wide variety of signals using only a computer and specialized software. In this laboratory, blocks such as accumulator, differentiator, and statistical operators (mean, RMS, quadratic mean, power spectral density, and standard deviation) were implemented in Python within GNU Radio. These tools allow the analysis of digital signals in real time and serve as a basis for advanced applications in telecommunications.  

---

## II. Methodology
The practice was divided into three stages:  

1. **Repository Management in GitHub:** Creation of the main branch *Practica_1* and organization of required folders. Git commands were used to manage and synchronize changes with the cloud.  

2. **Implementation in GNU Radio:** Python blocks were programmed following the laboratory guide. Functions such as accumulator, differentiator, and statistical operators were tested using blocks like *Vector Source*, *QT GUI Time Sink* and *QT GUI Number Sink*.  

3. **Documentation:** The results and contributions were integrated collaboratively in this report hosted in the GitHub repository.  

