## III. Results

### A. Accumulator  
The accumulator estimates the total energy in a signal window and reduces noise since the sum of many random samples tends to cancel unwanted variations.  

- **Fig. 1:** Flow diagram of the accumulator.  
- **Fig. 2–4:** Input/output signals with variations in the number of samples and sampling frequency.  

It was observed that the accumulator generates a ramp depending on the amplitude of the input square signal. Increasing the number of samples modifies the shape due to memory effect, while changing the sampling frequency mainly alters the speed of data acquisition without affecting amplitude.  

### B. Differentiator  
The differentiator emphasizes rapid changes in the input signal and behaves like a high-pass filter by attenuating low-frequency components.  

- **Fig. 5:** Flow diagram of the differentiator.  
- **Fig. 6–7:** Input/output signals with different numbers of samples.  

When the number of samples increased, pulses were more spaced; with fewer samples, changes occurred one after another, since the differentiator depends on differences between adjacent samples.  

